  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 330 AM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nChilly and unsettled weather will linger through the weekend, as \nmodels are in fairly good agreement showing that ridging will not \nimmediately fill back in over the area this time. The flow aloft \nremains zonal-ish, with a chilly surface high over the northern \nPlains preventing us from realizing a small increase in 700-mb \ntemperatures on Sunday. A few isolated snow showers will be possible \nboth Saturday and Sunday, but QPF expectations are minimal. Mostly, \nit will actually feel like November for a change, with highs in the \nupper 30s to lower 40s to most. This will actually be a few degrees \ncolder than seasonal averages. \n\nThe storm track will remain active into next week, with several \nupper level shortwave troughs continuing to move inland off of the \nPacific. However, nearly all ensembles continue to show these \nsystems falling apart by the time they reach our area, and thus \nthere is not really a clear signal for notable snowfall anytime \nsoon. The next trough will arrive around the Monday to Tuesday time \nframe, and may bring some light snow to portions of the area, though \na quarter of ensemble members are completely dry. Confidence is \nhigher that it will at least reinforce the chilly air, such that \nMonday and Tuesday are expected to be the coldest days of the week. \nTemperatures should recover somewhat on Wednesday and Thursday \n(Thanksgiving Day), but we should still be fairly close to seasonal \naverages. Another upper level trough may traverse the Rockies around \nThanksgiving, but at least 50% of ensemble members bring this one \nthrough completely dry as well.",
